---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CaloriesBurnedCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/CaloriesBurnedCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="calories-burned-calculator">
  <CaloriesBurnedCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the calories burned calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The calories burned calculator uses MET values. A MET (metabolic equivalent) is the energy cost of an
      activity as a multiple of the cost of sitting at rest. Brisk walking at 4.3 MET uses 4.3 times as much
      energy as rest. The values come from the 2011 Compendium of Physical Activities, a research list of
      over 800 activities.
    </p>
    <p>
      The formula is <code>kcal = MET × 3.5 × kg ÷ 200 × minutes</code>. One MET is 3.5 ml of oxygen per kg
      of body weight per minute, and each litre of oxygen releases about 5 kcal, which gives the 200. The
      energy above rest is the same formula with <code>MET − 1</code>.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A person who weighs 70 kg walks briskly at 3.5 mph (4.3 MET) for 30 minutes:
      4.3 × 3.5 × 70 ÷ 200 × 30 = 158 kcal, or 316 kcal an hour. Of the 158 kcal, 121 kcal are above what
      the person would burn at rest. Running at 6 mph (9.8 MET) for the same 30 minutes burns 360 kcal, and
      Hatha yoga (2.5 MET) burns 92 kcal.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      MET values are adult averages. Fitness, skill, terrain, wind and rest breaks change the real cost. The
      formula sets rest at 3.5 ml of oxygen per kg per minute; many people, most of all older or heavier
      people, use less at rest, so for them the result is too high. The total includes the energy you would
      burn at rest in the same time.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
